-- title:
--   Local Hecke algebra generated over 𝒪 by Hecke operators
-- statement:
--   Fix a level $N \ge 1$ and a set $S$ of natural numbers, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation domain which is complete with respect to its maximal-ideal adic filtration. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N)))$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N$ with $q \notin S$, to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$. Assume [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N 2`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6): the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ spanned by the forms all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers is the whole space. Let $\mathbb{T}_\theta =$ [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) be the localisation of the base-changed Hecke lattice algebra `heckeBaseAlgebra N S 𝒪` at the image under `latticeBaseChange` of the prime complement of `heckeCharKernel N S 𝒪 θ`, and let [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151) be the canonical map of the Hecke algebra into $\mathbb{T}_\theta$. The conclusion is that the $\mathcal{O}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ generated by the range of this map is the whole of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$. The assertion concerns generation as an algebra only; no statement is made about module generation or about sufficiency of finitely many operators.
--
--   This is the generation statement for the local Hecke algebra attached to a mod-$\mathfrak{m}$ Hecke eigensystem $\theta$: homomorphisms out of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ are determined by their values on the Hecke operators. It is used throughout the construction of Hecke coefficient rings and of the Galois representations attached to $\theta$, for instance in producing a representation with prescribed local behaviour at the primes dividing the level.
